So, once the rating exceeds $\max(R_i)$, it stops increasing.
Let $M = \max(R_i)$. If the rating reaches $M+1$, it stays constant for all subsequent contests.
Actually, the maximum value of $R_i$ is $5 \times 10^5$. So if the rating is $> 5 \times 10^5$, it will never increase again.
Let's call the maximum possible value of $R_i$ as $MAX\_R = 500000$.
If the initial rating $X > MAX\_R$, then for all contests $i$, $L_i \le R_i \le MAX\_R < X$. So the condition $L_i \le \text{rating} \le R_i$ will never be met. The rating will remain $X$.
So for $X > 500000$, the answer is just $X$.

For $X \le 500000$, the rating might increase.
Since the rating increases by at most 1 per contest, and there are $N$ contests, the rating can increase by at most $N$.
